body:
Scaffold refresh: the first half of `kalide upgrade`. Kalide-owned scaffold content is the content kalide itself writes into a project and still owns — the two embedded `AGENTS.md` author guides (the deck guide, agent-authoring, and the library guide, library-agent-guide) and the minimal `kalide init` seed tree (cli-init-minimal-seed: the seed `templates/library.yaml`, the `hello` slide template with its `example.md`, the seed default theme `templates/themes/default/theme.css`, and the starter slide `slides/1-hello.md`). The scaffolding commands write this content once, and every scaffold writer writes the `AGENTS.md` guide only when none exists (writeHelloSeed in internal/scaffold/helloseed.go, InitExternal in external.go, InitLibrary in library.go), so a project scaffolded by an older kalide carries that release's guide. The build-time agent-guide-drift-check keeps the embedded guide in step with the format the code implements — the reserved `media:`/`theme:<name>/` theme-CSS prefixes and library-wide CSS reach (theme-media-references) are carried by both — and upgrade is the runtime counterpart of that check.

A refresh member is rewritten only when it is present and byte-identical to a known released kalide version of that same file (global.constraint.upgrade-known-version-catalog). The `AGENTS.md` author guides are the one exception: they are also written when absent, because a missing guide is exactly the drift this feature exists to fix and the guide is inert documentation. Seed starter content — the seed `templates/library.yaml`, the `hello` template under `templates/slides/hello/`, the seed default theme `templates/themes/default/theme.css` and the starter slide `slides/1-hello.md`, plus a library's seeded `themes/default/theme.css` — is never re-created when it is absent: an author who deleted it after init (the normal thing to do) has that deletion respected, and upgrade must never introduce a new starter file that would change the deck. A present file whose bytes match no known released version — because the author edited it, or because it is not a kalide scaffold file — is treated as author content: it is left byte-for-byte untouched and reported, never overwritten, whatever the format migration would otherwise do.

The set is scoped to the project's scaffold form and never grows. Which deck owns the seed tree is an objective predicate, not a marker: a deck is a no-arg seed deck exactly when its `kalide.yaml` carries no non-empty `templates:` key (deck.TemplatesPath(cfg) is "") and a local `<deck root>/templates` directory exists. A deck whose `templates:` key is non-empty is an external-library deck; when such a deck also has a local `templates/` directory, that directory is a library layer loaded alongside the external library into the deck's one library (layered-template-library), and upgrade treats it as author content, never as the seed tree. Only a no-arg seed deck owns the seed tree, and the seed-owned paths are exactly `templates/library.yaml`, `templates/slides/hello/*`, `templates/themes/default/theme.css` and `slides/1-hello.md`; an external-library deck's local `templates/` layer is author content, so upgrade never creates a local `templates/` or a starter slide that the deck does not already have, and a library owns its library-root guide and its seeded default theme, never templates or media. `kalide.yaml` is the deck config the author edits (title, author, date, properties) and is not a whole-file refresh candidate; its structure is migration territory, not refresh territory. Every refreshed and every left-alone path is reported.

AC:
- a deck scaffolded by a previous kalide with an unmodified old `AGENTS.md` ends with the current guide after upgrade, and the project still serves (agent-authoring);
- an `AGENTS.md` guide that is absent from a deck or library is written; an author-edited `AGENTS.md` is left byte-for-byte unchanged and reported;
- a deleted starter slide (`slides/1-hello.md`), a deleted `hello` template (`templates/slides/hello/`) and a deleted seed default theme (`templates/themes/default/theme.css`) are not re-created, and a deck the author left valid still serves with identical rendered output before and after upgrade;
- an external-library deck is not given a local `templates/` directory or a starter slide it never had;
- a seed file that is present and byte-identical to a known released version is refreshed to the current content; the same file once the author has edited it is left untouched and reported;
- author slides, sections, templates, themes and media are byte-for-byte identical before and after;
- the command output lists every refreshed path and every path left alone.
